// @run
// @log hi
// @log 9
// D1597 — A MODULE GLOBAL AND A SAME-NAMED BINDING IN A TOP-LEVEL BLOCK ARE ONE SLOT.
// Every module's top-level statements are lowered into one start function, so a `const`
// written inside a top-level block is a start-function LOCAL. Wasm locals are
// FUNCTION-scoped and VL's module top level is LEXICALLY scoped, so the read below —
// outside the block, of the module global — was claimed by the block's local.
//
// This file was the LOUD face. The same collision at a numeric global was a check-clean
// SILENTLY WRONG VALUE (`scripts/capability-probes/module-global-read-claimed-by-block-binding.vl`),
// and struct-on-struct was check-clean invalid wasm (`uninitialized local: 0`).
//
// THE CONTROL IS A SECOND MODULE: `tests/cases/modules/module-global-shadowed-by-block-binding-cross-module/`
// is this program with one import added, and it ran and printed the right values all along.
// The module merge mangles TOP-LEVEL names per module (`n` -> `n$m1`) and leaves block-local
// ones alone, so the collision only existed in a program with no imports at all — which is
// why every cross-module fixture in the tree missed it. The fix is NOT the mangling: a
// block-scoped slot is marked at its declaration and the flat registry scan passes it over
// wherever the walk's own scope stack has already had its say.
//
// The read from INSIDE the block is the direction the fix must not break: there `n` DOES
// denote the block binding, so it prints `hi` before the global's `9`.
if true {
  const n = "hi"
  print(n)
}

const n = { base: 9 }
print(n.base)
